= Overview =

PKI provides CLI to manage user certificates. All user certificate commands must be executed as the subsystem administrator.

= Commands =

== Listing user certificates ==

<pre>
$ pki <admin authentication> <subsystem>-user-find-cert <user ID>
</pre>

For example:

<pre>
$ pki -d ~/.dogtag/pki-tomcat/ca/alias/ -c Secret.123 -n caadmin ca-user-cert-find caadmin
-----------------
1 entries matched
-----------------
  Cert ID: 2;6;CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE;CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE
  Version: 2
  Serial Number: 0x6
  Issuer: CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE
  Subject: CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE
----------------------------
Number of entries returned 1
</pre>

== Viewing a user certificate ==

<pre>
$ pki <admin authentication> <subsystem>-user-cert-show <user ID> <certificate ID>
</pre>

For example:

<pre>
$ pki -d ~/.dogtag/pki-tomcat/ca/alias/ -c Secret.123 -n caadmin ca-user-cert-show caadmin "2;6;CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE;CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE"
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Certificate "2;6;CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE;CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE"
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Cert ID: 2;6;CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE;CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE
  Version: 2
  Serial Number: 0x6
  Issuer: CN=CA Signing Certificate,O=EXAMPLE
  Subject: CN=PKI Administrator,E=caadmin@example.com,O=EXAMPLE
</pre>

== Exporting user certificate ==

<pre>
$ pki <admin authentication> <subsystem>-user-cert-show <user ID> <certificate ID> --output <filename>
</pre>

== Importing user certificate from file ==

<pre>
$ pki <admin authentication> <subsystem>-user-cert-add <user ID> --input <filename>
</pre>

